<main class="methods-main">
  <section class="methods-hero"><p class="eyebrow">TRANSPARENCY WITHOUT THE DETOUR</p><h1>Methods, limitations &amp; downloads</h1><p class="deck">The public evidence, survey instrument, analysis rules, code, and aggregate outputs live here, separate from the main community story.</p><a class="ghost-button" href="./">← Back to the report</a></section>
  <section class="method-section panel"><h2>Source, timing, and privacy boundary</h2><ul><li><strong>Project author:</strong> JVMES, a participant in the referenced Druid Classic Discord discussion.</li><li><strong>Authoritative source:</strong> a private export of the survey response dataset maintained by the project author. The live response sheet is not publicly linked. Verification materials may be considered upon request, subject to respondent privacy and consent; raw respondent data are not publicly distributed.</li><li><strong>Collection period:</strong> {{ collection_start }} through {{ collection_end }} America/Chicago.</li><li><strong>Retrieved:</strong> {{ retrieved_display }}.</li><li><strong>Snapshot:</strong> {{ metadata.row_count }} populated timestamp rows × {{ metadata.column_count }} columns.</li><li><strong>Snapshot fingerprint (SHA-256):</strong> <code>{{ metadata.sha256 }}</code>. This can confirm that a rerun used the same private export. It does not authenticate the collection process or reveal the responses.</li><li><strong>Reproducible source:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/JVMES-WOW/forever-druid/tree/main/survey_analysis">analysis code and documentation on GitHub</a>.</li><li><strong>Privacy:</strong> raw responses and local snapshots are gitignored. Only aggregates, anonymous model weights, and permission-checked excerpts are public.</li></ul><p><strong>Design reference boundary:</strong> the survey asked about the “currently revealed” design available during the collection window; it did not collect a patch or build identifier. Results should be read as reactions to the public and test-build state respondents encountered on those dates, not to later revisions.</p><p>The local project also contains DiscordKit exports of the Druid Classic channels <strong>#forever-general</strong>, <strong>#forever-feral-dps</strong>, and <strong>#forever-feral-tank</strong>, covering discussion from September 12–20, 2026. A <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/classicwow/comments/1wljuy6/">public r/classicwow announcement</a> documents how the survey was framed and circulated. These materials establish the author’s perspective and project context only. They are not part of the analyzed corpus, quoted, or counted.</p></section>
  <section class="method-section"><h2>Survey instrument and interpretation boundary</h2><p>The exact wording and response formats are provided below. Q8 contains an answer choice that explicitly names “comparable activity and meaningful resource decisions,” so Q8 alone cannot independently establish that those properties matter. The main interpretation is triangulated across the importance matrix (Q9), current-design agreement matrix (Q10), forced-priority selection (Q12), test-direction selection (Q13), and written responses (Q14–Q16).</p><details class="instrument"><summary>View all question wording and response options</summary><div class="table-scroll">{{ html_tables.survey_instrument|safe }}</div></details></section>
  <section class="method-section"><h2>Sample and data quality</h2><p>Valid responses are rows with populated timestamps, not allocated sheet rows. The snapshot contains <strong>{{ metadata.exact_duplicate_rows }} exact duplicate answer rows</strong> and <strong>{{ metadata.duplicate_timestamp_rows }} rows sharing a timestamp</strong>. Shared timestamps are possible in a rapid-response survey and are not treated as duplicates without matching answers; all were retained. “Not enough information” remains in every stated denominator.</p><div class="table-scroll">{{ html_tables.missingness|safe }}</div></section>
  <section class="method-section"><h2>Subgroup comparisons</h2><p>Subgroup results are descriptive, not causal. Groups under 30 are omitted. Major proportions include Wilson 95% confidence intervals, which describe uncertainty within this self-selected sample rather than making it representative.</p><figure><img src="figures/subgroups.svg" alt="Dot and interval plot comparing negative reactions across major subgroups."><figcaption>Denominators appear in each label.</figcaption></figure><div class="table-scroll">{{ html_tables.subgroups|safe }}</div></section>
  <section class="method-section"><h2>Written-response patterns</h2><p>The three questions were modeled separately. Phrase-cloud sizes use the number of distinct responses mentioning a two- or three-word phrase, not repetition inside one response. Generic fragments and survey boilerplate were removed, while domain phrases such as “Tiger’s Fury,” “energy pooling,” “skill expression,” and “Bear weaving” stay intact.</p><div class="cloud-grid"><figure><img src="figures/wordcloud-q14.png" alt="Phrase cloud for Q14, sized by distinct response mentions."><figcaption>Q14 · What developers should understand</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="figures/wordcloud-q15.png" alt="Phrase cloud for Q15, sized by distinct response mentions."><figcaption>Q15 · What should be preserved</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="figures/wordcloud-q16.png" alt="Phrase cloud for Q16, sized by distinct response mentions."><figcaption>Q16 · One requested change</figcaption></figure></div><h3>Topic modeling</h3><p>TF-IDF unigrams and bigrams feed deterministic non-negative matrix factorization. Candidate solutions from 4–10 topics were inspected for stability, repeated components, top terms, and high-weight responses. Labels are human interpretations of both the terms and representative comments. Primary-topic shares are not survey vote percentages.</p><figure><img src="figures/topic-prevalence.svg" alt="Bar chart of exclusive primary-topic shares for Q14, Q15, and Q16."><figcaption>One primary topic per response for display; comments can contain several themes.</figcaption></figure><h3>Manual thematic and polarity check</h3><p>Rule-based mention coding provides a second, transparent layer for the core themes. Categories overlap. Ambiguous comments are not forced into a position. Q15 powershifting mentions are separately checked because a topic model cannot reliably distinguish “preserve powershifting” from “preserve its removal.”</p><div class="table-scroll">{{ html_tables.thematic_validation|safe }}</div><div class="table-scroll">{{ html_tables.q15_powershift_polarity|safe }}</div></section>
  <section class="method-section"><h2>Limitations</h2><ul><li>This is a self-selected sample of engaged Feral players recruited through community channels over a short period.</li><li>Community-channel reach, question wording, response dependence, and an evolving test build limit generalization.</li><li>No combat logs, telemetry, parses, account history, or externally verified skill measures were collected.</li><li>The survey measures reactions and design priorities, not whether a proposed mechanic will balance correctly in raids, dungeons, leveling, or PvP.</li><li>Topic models summarize language; they do not measure votes and are weak at negation.</li><li>Rule-based themes favor transparency over perfect recall and should be read as mentions, not exhaustive hand coding.</li><li>Subgroup differences are associations inside this sample, not causal effects.</li></ul></section>
  <section class="method-section" id="downloads"><h2>Aggregate downloads</h2><p>Multiselect files explicitly note that percentages may exceed 100%. No raw response export is included.</p><div class="download-grid">{% for name in ["source_metadata.json","survey_instrument.csv","overall_direction.csv","likelihood_to_play.csv","excitement.csv","powershifting_preference.csv","familiarity.csv","primary_interest.csv","experience.csv","versions_played.csv","developer_attention.csv","directions_to_test.csv","importance_matrix.csv","agreement_matrix.csv","mechanic_reactions.csv","subgroups.csv","thematic_validation.csv","q15_powershift_polarity.csv","topic_prevalence.csv","topic_terms_q14.csv","topic_terms_q15.csv","topic_terms_q16.csv","topic_assignments_q14.csv","topic_assignments_q15.csv","topic_assignments_q16.csv","topic_diagnostics_q14.csv","topic_diagnostics_q15.csv","topic_diagnostics_q16.csv","wordcloud_q14_terms.csv","wordcloud_q15_terms.csv","wordcloud_q16_terms.csv"] %}<a href="tables/{{ name }}">{{ name }}</a>{% endfor %}</div></section>
